+# Hermes Kanban value props
+
+## Core message
+Hermes Kanban turns multi-agent work from a pile of separate terminal sessions into one visible, coordinated board. Users describe the goal once; specialist agents claim the right cards, work in parallel, hand off dependencies, and pause when blocked. The human stays in control from the board: watch progress, inspect handoffs, unblock decisions, and see the whole workflow move from idea to finished artifact.
+
+## Announcement takeaways
+- Multi-agent execution is organized through Kanban, not ad hoc terminal juggling.
+- Agents claim tasks from the shared board, so work can be routed to the right specialist profile.
+- Independent tasks run in parallel, while dependent tasks wait for parent handoffs.
+- Blocked agents hand the decision back to the user instead of silently guessing or stalling.
+- The board is the control plane: users can monitor status, review outputs, unblock tasks, and understand the entire pipeline in one place.
+
+## Value propositions
+1. One board replaces many terminals.
+   - Before: launch agents manually, track separate processes, copy/paste context between shells.
+   - With Hermes Kanban: the board shows who is working, what is blocked, what is done, and what is next.
+
+2. Parallel specialist work without coordination overhead.
+   - Research, design, coding, review, and editing lanes can run simultaneously when dependencies allow.
+   - Fan-in tasks receive structured handoffs from completed parent cards.
+
+3. Safe human-in-the-loop autonomy.
+   - Agents can move quickly on clear tasks.
+   - When a decision genuinely needs human input, they block with a concrete question and wait for unblocking.
+
+4. Durable handoffs instead of ephemeral chat context.
+   - Each card keeps the task body, comments, run history, summaries, metadata, and artifacts.
+   - Downstream agents inherit the relevant parent results without the user re-explaining the project.
+
+5. Observable progress for complex workflows.
+   - The user can see the whole production pipeline at a glance: pending, running, blocked, review-needed, complete.
+   - This makes multi-agent execution feel like managing a team, not babysitting processes.
+
+## Suggested concise announcement line
+"Hermes Kanban is a control board for multi-agent work: agents claim cards, run in parallel, hand off through dependencies, block for human decisions, and let you steer the whole workflow from one place instead of juggling terminals."
+
+## Suggested video/voiceover phrasing
+"Instead of opening five terminals and trying to remember which agent is doing what, drop the project onto a Hermes Kanban board. Research and design can run side by side. Animation waits for both handoffs. Reviewers see the finished cut. If an agent gets blocked, it asks on the card and you unblock it from the same board. Multi-agent work becomes visible, coordinated, and controllable."
+
+## Keywords to emphasize
+- one board, many agents
+- claim tasks
+- parallel work
+- dependency handoffs
+- blocked/unblocked by the user
+- no terminal juggling
+- visible control plane
